WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.160
Let’s talk about how far we’ve 

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:03.520
come with this coronavirus.

00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:05.880
And how much more we still have to figure out.

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:09.720
Since the very start of the outbreak

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:10.920
and throughout the pandemic 

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:12.560
there’s been a lot of debate, 

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:14.600
a lot of science

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:15.640
and for some of us 

00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:16.840
a lot of confusion.

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:26.800
There’s plenty we’re still learning

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:28.880
from how we avoid getting sick 

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:30.880
to how we deal with it when we do. 

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:32.800
And what the future looks like.

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:40.000
So here’s our most recent episode on COVID-19 

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:41.440
updated with expert guidance

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:43.320
to help us understand the pandemic.

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:44.680
And stay safe.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:53.960
Science is always evolving.

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:55.600
It’s just how science works.

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:57.800
Someone does a study, publishes it

00:00:57.800 --> 00:00:59.960
then others either confirm the results

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:01.920
or point out the mistakes. 

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:03.400
But that all takes time.

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:04.800
And when the pandemic started

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:06.880
time wasn’t on anyone’s side.

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:24.920
There was a lot of uncertainty back then 

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:27.840
and governments had to rely on the information available.

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:30.560
And the main body that’s been putting that information out

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:32.840
is the UN’s World Health Organization. 

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:34.440
So we spoke to their lead scientist

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:37.920
dealing with the coronavirus, known as SARS-CoV-2, 

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:39.760
to take us through what we knew then 

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:41.480
and what we know now.

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:56.440
We’ll get to superspreading events later. 

00:01:56.440 --> 00:02:00.120
But first let’s go over how this virus behaves.

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:01.880
From the moment a person is infected 

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:05.560
it can take up to fourteen days for symptoms to appear.

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:08.720
But usually it’s between five and six days.

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:12.040
Some people can infect others just before they get symptoms. 

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:14.960
This is known as presymptomatic transmission.

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:18.880
Other people never develop symptoms but can still spread the virus.

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:21.360
This is called asymptomatic transmission 

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:23.520
but it doesn’t appear to be as common.

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:26.160
It’s why we have to act like everyone’s sick 

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:29.920
because a lot of the time we just don’t know who is.

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:31.840
Now, the WHO says that on average 

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:35.400
a person spreads the virus to two or three other people.

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:37.360
It’s what’s called the reproduction number.

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:39.040
It can be less. 

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:40.920
That means some people don’t pass the virus

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:42.360
to others at all. 

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:45.920
But it can also be a lot higher under certain conditions.

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:48.240
That’s when you have a superspreading event.

00:02:58.600 --> 00:02:59.560
What seems to be happening 

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:01.920
is that a small number of infected people

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:03.600
about ten to twenty percent 

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:07.840
are responsible for around eighty percent of transmission events. 

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:11.120
For example a singer in the US state of Washington

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:14.320
infected 53 people at a choir practice.

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:17.600
There was superspreading at a megachurch in Seoul.

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:19.480
At a football game in Italy.

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:21.040
And it’s still happening.

00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:41.320
Scientists have always known that respiratory viruses 

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:43.520
spread through small particles.

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:45.200
It’s what we explained back in April.

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:48.240
You can catch it from someone coughing, sneezing 

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:51.400
or speaking a little too close to you.

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:53.760
But what if the droplets linger

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:57.920
Well those particles can linger on surfaces and in the air.

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:01.040
If particles are larger they tend to travel a certain distance

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:04.000
let’s say about a metre, and then fall to the ground.

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:05.680
Scientists call these droplets.

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:09.920
But if they’re smaller they can stay suspended in the air for longer

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:12.360
and potentially travel much further.

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:14.480
Scientists call these aerosols.

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:15.960
So if she has COVID-19 

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:19.120
and she’s talking to me from across the room

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:20.880
There’s poor ventilation here.

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:22.960
and neither of us is wearing a mask

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:24.720
the aerosols might  still reach me.

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:27.200
And that could infect me if enough of the virus

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:30.480
reaches my eyes, nose or mouth.

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:32.520
It all depends on the environment.

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:34.520
Scientists talk about the three C’s

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:37.240
close interactions, crowded areas 

00:04:37.240 --> 00:04:40.240
and closed off spaces without proper ventilation.

00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:57.320
But no matter the environment the advice is the same. 

00:04:58.360 --> 00:04:59.720
Things like washing your hands

00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:02.080
keeping a distance of at least a metre 

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:03.760
and wearing a mask.

00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:06.120
But there are still people who refuse to wear masks.

00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:19.200
Now Children can spread this virus. 

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:22.120
But for some reason they don’t seem to get as sick.

00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:26.240
And if children do get it they’re way less likely to die.

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:29.160
By mid-July in the US the Centers for Disease Control 

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:32.880
said the virus had killed more than 150,000 people 

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:36.920
but only seventeen of them were under the age of five.

00:05:36.920 --> 00:05:39.240
But a few young people who got COVID-19 

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:42.080
have also come down with a mystery illness.

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:44.200
They’re calling it PMIS.

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:04.920
So lots of research is still happening. 

00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:06.880
Particularly with vaccines.

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:08.120
The numbers keep changing 

00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:11.960
but there are about 170 vaccines in development. 

00:06:11.960 --> 00:06:14.040
Close to thirty are being tested on people.

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:18.760
Russia says it’s already rolling out its vaccine

00:06:18.760 --> 00:06:20.480
called Sputnik V.

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:22.640
But Germany’s warning against rushing through 

00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:25.240
approvals and says a vaccine won’t be ready

00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:28.080
to give to everyone before next year.

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:30.360
We just have to sit tight.

00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:48.560
In the meantime

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:51.480
we have treatments that doctors say are helping.

00:06:52.040 --> 00:06:54.480
Like steroids which can reduce the chances

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:58.200
of someone dying from COVID-19 by twenty percent.

00:06:58.200 --> 00:07:00.720
But the WHO recommends them only for patients 

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:02.200
who are very sick.

00:07:02.200 --> 00:07:06.000
They say they can actually be harmful if used for mild cases.

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:09.880
There’s other stuff scientists are still working out. 

00:07:09.880 --> 00:07:13.920
Like why COVID-19 can be so tough on some people.

00:07:49.960 --> 00:07:52.160
So there’s a lot more to figure out

00:07:52.160 --> 00:07:54.360
But all anyone really wants to know is

00:07:54.360 --> 00:07:57.120
when can our lives go back to normal.

00:07:57.120 --> 00:08:00.240
The WHO says that's already possible 

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:01.720
but that’s a new normal.

00:08:01.720 --> 00:08:03.920
We have to take the right precautions.

00:08:03.920 --> 00:08:07.200
But they say we don’t need anymore countrywide lockdowns.

00:08:24.520 --> 00:08:27.320
So we know what we need to do to stay safe.

00:08:27.320 --> 00:08:30.360
And we know a lot more about how this virus spreads. 

00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:33.440
Now we’ve just gotta learn to live with it.

00:08:34.600 --> 00:08:35.560
Science evolves.

00:08:35.560 --> 00:08:39.120
And the guidance on COVID-19 gets updated all the time. 

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:40.960
So the best way to stay informed

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:43.360
is to access those organisations that research

00:08:43.360 --> 00:08:45.560
this stuff day in and day out 

00:08:45.560 --> 00:08:48.960
like the WHO and others who issue health advice.

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:52.000
Stay safe and I’ll see you next week.

